    "notes": "PROVISIONAL. Broad jump norms for adults over 30 are essentially unpublished - the widely circulated ratings tables are for 16-19 year olds and carry no age adjustment. The anchors, 78 inches for men and 58 for women at age 42, sit around what those tables call average-to-above-average for young adults, discounted for age. The 7.5 per cent per decade decline follows published jump-power decline, which is steeper than strength decline. The 0.14 coefficient of variation comes from reported jump-test variability, not from a normative sample.",
